#edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416252 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416252 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416252 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416252 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2a61099-7c6c-5889-a8f0-36c27d613946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416253 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e533a5eb-946c-53fd-b9db-7dca4ec6140a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416254 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ecf8557a-f127-5151-95f4-647459864ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416255 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee421f31-693d-5e9b-9b13-f8f8930e49f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416256 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f0260c4e-12d5-5629-b2d4-7c1539134366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0be1c169-3c4d-5e81-bcd5-f050f3ef61f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416257 #edb/ref 1074 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})-(8(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{4})) \\\\  & =16-4-8+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{3}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+2t)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "115c519a-042d-5002-84f6-473dd06e56ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416258 #edb/ref 1074 "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+{0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=2+{2}^{2}=6\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dec6491-4f2e-5985-9f0d-5f140c0cfaf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416259 #edb/ref 1074 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+1|+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+1|=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+1|=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8{ln}^{2}⁡2-2{ln}^{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{3}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34ad571d-a040-5724-a4dd-5d1ff6b4a0c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416260 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{3}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+2) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=3(2)^{2}=12\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)^{2}=27\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (27)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (12)^{2} \\\\  & =729-144 \\\\  & =585\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49e23a3c-f5ee-5f32-8207-650dc0b4ca07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416261 #edb/ref 1074 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{1/2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3/2}+2x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2})+2(4)-(2({1}^{3/2})+2(1)) \\\\  & =16+8-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $0.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "539e0059-24fe-5c3a-b9b6-abd2934de330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416262 #edb/ref 1074 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $0.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=0.5\\cdot (5-x)=(2.5-0.5x)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2.5-0.5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{2})-(\\frac{5}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =5-1-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(3t-1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6813bd80-c67c-56a5-8d0d-2e4e5ee1b618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416263 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=3t-1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=3(1)-1=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)-1=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (8)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =96-6 \\\\  & =90\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3\\sqrt{t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a6ac8ce-563c-538e-a4b6-829308c43be7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416264 #edb/ref 1074 "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=2({0}^{3/2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3/2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=2({1}^{3/2})=2\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841964b0-d48f-583c-9b1a-d5d38816deaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416265 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16+16-0 \\\\  & =32\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =50-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $3\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-3=2\\,m/s.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a80bea3f-8cb9-59ff-8c63-3325a8f0a338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416266 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(12+\\frac{1}{4})-(3+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{4}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{49}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-4=3\\,m/s.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2-x)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc2329ee-2286-5cc1-b9c4-aaae4fcfd160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416267 #edb/ref 1074 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2})-(2(1)-\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =4-2-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $0.25\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d148a18c-482f-58b6-ae26-c16e793a32c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416268 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=4(1)^{3}-4(1)=0\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=4(2)^{3}-4(2)=24\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot (24)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =72-0 \\\\  & =72\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $3{x}^{2}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e08604c1-4714-50ad-b1df-f5621c3586f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416269 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{v}_{1}^{2}-8 \\\\ 2{v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=3\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdc532fe-5376-523b-b4c1-4852cb3ef3ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416270 #edb/ref 1074 "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-\\frac{1}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-\\frac{1}{0+1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1-\\frac{1}{t+1}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(3)=1-\\frac{1}{3+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
